---
title: Marks and Layering
description: Choose built-in geometric marks, compose them in render order, and preserve stable layer identity.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A mark turns data and channel values into renderer-neutral scene nodes. Marks are small, composable units; a chart type is usually a useful arrangement of several marks rather than a separate component."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"built-in-mark-families","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Built-in mark families"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Visual task"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Trend or connected path"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lineY"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Difference between two connected paths"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"differenceY"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"differenceX"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Least-squares trend and confidence band"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"linearRegressionY"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Range, band, or filled trend"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"areaY"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"areaX"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Category comparison"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"barY"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"barX"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interval, heatmap cell, or rectangular region"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rect"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Relationship or individual observation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dot"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hexagon"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tukey distribution summary"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"boxY"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"boxX"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Baseline, threshold, or reference"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ruleX"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ruleY"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Label or annotation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"text"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Directed relationship"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"arrow"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"link"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"vector"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compact distribution glyph"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tickX"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tickY"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Plot frame"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"frame"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Small-multiple composition"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"facet"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"facetChart"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pie, donut, gauge, or cyclic profile"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"polar"},{"type":"text","value":" and radial marks"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Projected GeoJSON"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"geoShape"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start from the analytical question in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/choosing-a-chart.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choosing a Chart"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/index.md#mark-reference","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mark Reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":" lists every channel and\nstyle option. Polar and geographic marks use explicit capability subpaths."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"layer-order-is-declaration-order","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layer order is declaration order"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marks earlier in the array paint behind later marks:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const marks = [\n  areaY(rangeRows, rangeOptions),\n  ruleY([target], ruleOptions),\n  lineY(actualRows, lineOptions),\n  dot(highlightedRows, dotOptions),\n  text(labels, textOptions),\n]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A useful default order is:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Background regions and filled areas"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reference bands and rules"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Primary bars or lines"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Highlight dots, ticks, or vectors"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Labels and annotations"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There is no separate overlay subsystem. An annotation is another mark with its own data, channels, and stable identity."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"opt-individual-marks-into-canvas","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Opt individual marks into Canvas"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import the Canvas renderer and attach it only to paint-heavy marks:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { canvasChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/canvas'\n\nconst marks = [\n  areaY(denseRange, {\n    x: 'date',\n    y1: 'low',\n    y2: 'high',\n    renderer: canvasChartRenderer,\n  }),\n  lineY(summary, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n  dot(highlights, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n  text(labels, { x: 'date', y: 'value', text: 'label' }),\n]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The host keeps axes, guides, and marks without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"renderer"},{"type":"text","value":" in SVG. It creates\nordered SVG and Canvas layers from the mark declaration order, so a Canvas\nmark can sit behind, between, or in front of SVG marks. Focus, tooltips,\nkeyboard navigation, responsive updates, SSR shell adoption, and export still\nuse the shared chart host."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cartesian and radial mark option objects accept "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"renderer"},{"type":"text","value":". A\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"compositeMark"},{"type":"text","value":" can select a renderer for its complete output, or its children\ncan select their own renderers when the parent does not. The same nested\nselection works inside facets and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"polar"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Importing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/canvas"},{"type":"text","value":" is the opt-in boundary that adds the Canvas\npainter to the bundle. Reuse a stable renderer instance across updates. The\nexported "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"canvasChartRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":" singleton already has stable identity. Creating\na new renderer or changing the renderer sequence replaces the affected layer\ncomposition."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"decorative-layers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Decorative layers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When two layered marks describe the same observations, choose one interaction\nowner. For example, dots can own focus and tooltips while the connected line\nremains visual:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { dot, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { decorative } from '@tanstack/charts/mark/decorative'\n\nconst marks = [\n  decorative(lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' })),\n  dot(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"decorative(mark)"},{"type":"text","value":" preserves the mark's scale channels, domains, layout-label\nmeasurement, motion, and painted geometry. It removes interaction points and\nscene ownership, so the layer cannot add a second keyboard stop, tooltip, or\nactivation target. The input must be an always-painted mark without focus or\nstate behavior."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"mark-identity","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mark identity"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every mark has an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":". When omitted, it is derived from the mark type and array position."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provide an explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":" when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marks appear conditionally."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marks reorder."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two definitions should reconcile the same conceptual layer."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Application code needs a stable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"markId"},{"type":"text","value":" in interaction points."}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"lineY(rows, {\n  id: 'actual-revenue',\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'actual',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Built-in marks infer datum identity from a unique top-level "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":", nested\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data.id"},{"type":"text","value":", or mark-specific position. Supply "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" when that inferred value is\nnot the entity's stable identity."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"grouped-geometry","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouped geometry"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" partitions geometry that should not connect:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"lineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'value',\n  z: 'region',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each region becomes an independent line. Area marks use the same grouping\nrule. When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" is omitted on a connected line or area, an authored "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":"\nchannel supplies the path groups as well as color semantics. Explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":"\nalways wins when the two fields differ."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bars stack their single quantitative channel by default. Use\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"layout: group({ scale })"},{"type":"text","value":" for side-by-side bars; it groups by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" when present\nand otherwise by discrete "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":". Explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y1"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y2"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x1"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x2"},{"type":"text","value":" channels\nopt out of implicit stacking and preserve authored intervals."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"line-and-area-gaps","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Line and area gaps"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lineY"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"areaY"},{"type":"text","value":" split geometry at missing or invalid positional values:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface Reading {\n  id: string\n  time: Date\n  low: number | null\n  high: number | null\n}\n\nareaY(readings, {\n  x: 'time',\n  y1: 'low',\n  y2: 'high',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The break is intentional evidence that no interval was materialized for that observation. Do not replace missing data with zero unless zero is semantically correct."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"baselines-and-intervals","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Baselines and intervals"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bar and area marks accept explicit endpoints:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"areaY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y1: 'minimum',\n  y2: 'maximum',\n})\n\nbarY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y1: 'start',\n  y2: 'end',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y1"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x1"},{"type":"text","value":" is omitted, bar and area baselines default to zero where that mark supports it. Supplying both endpoints makes the interval semantics explicit and includes both sides in scale materialization."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rectangles are the general interval mark:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"rect(windows, {\n  x1: 'start',\n  x2: 'end',\n  y1: 'minimum',\n  y2: 'maximum',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"style-values-and-visual-channels","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Style values and visual channels"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Some appearance options are constants:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"lineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'value',\n  stroke: '#2563eb',\n  strokeWidth: 2.5,\n  strokeOpacity: 0.9,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marks that accept "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VisualChannel"},{"type":"text","value":" options can also derive a style from each row:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"barX(rows, {\n  x: 'value',\n  y: 'label',\n  fill: (row) => (row.highlighted ? '#f97316' : '#94a3b8'),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a semantic group or color channel and configured color scale when color represents data consistently across observations. Use a visual accessor for local styling that is not a shared scale."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"clipping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clipping"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clip: true"},{"type":"text","value":" on the chart definition when marks must not paint outside the resolved plot rectangle:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const chart = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  clip: true,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clipping applies to the chart’s mark group, not axes or legends. Leave it off when an intentional annotation or marker should extend beyond the plot."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"complete-range-band-composition","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete range-band composition"}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"temperature-range-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"temperature-range","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\\nimport { areaY, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\nimport { sfTemperatures } from './data'\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    areaY(sfTemperatures, {\\n      id: 'daily-range',\\n      x: 'date',\\n      y1: 'low',\\n      y2: 'high',\\n      fill: '#60a5fa',\\n      fillOpacity: 0.24,\\n    }),\\n    lineY(sfTemperatures, {\\n      id: 'daily-low',\\n      x: 'date',\\n      y: 'low',\\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\\n    }),\\n    lineY(sfTemperatures, {\\n      id: 'daily-high',\\n      x: 'date',\\n      y: 'high',\\n      stroke: '#dc2626',\\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: {\\n      scale: scaleUtc,\\n      axis: { label: 'Day' },\\n    },\\n    y: {\\n      scale: scaleLinear,\\n      nice: true,\\n      grid: true,\\n      axis: { label: 'Temperature (°F)' },\\n    },\\n  },\\n})\",\"/src/data.ts\":\"export interface DailyTemperature {\\n  date: Date\\n  high: number\\n  low: number\\n}\\n\\nexport const sfTemperatures: readonly DailyTemperature[] = [\\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-01T00:00:00Z'), high: 68, low: 55 },\\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-02T00:00:00Z'), high: 71, low: 56 },\\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-03T00:00:00Z'), high: 66, low: 54 },\\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-04T00:00:00Z'), high: 69, low: 55 },\\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-05T00:00:00Z'), high: 73, low: 57 },\\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-06T00:00:00Z'), high: 70, low: 56 },\\n]\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\nimport { areaY, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { sfTemperatures } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    areaY(sfTemperatures, {\n      id: 'daily-range',\n      x: 'date',\n      y1: 'low',\n      y2: 'high',\n      fill: '#60a5fa',\n      fillOpacity: 0.24,\n    }),\n    lineY(sfTemperatures, {\n      id: 'daily-low',\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'low',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n    lineY(sfTemperatures, {\n      id: 'daily-high',\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'high',\n      stroke: '#dc2626',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleUtc,\n      axis: { label: 'Day' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      nice: true,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Temperature (°F)' },\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=temperature-range env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"export interface DailyTemperature {\n  date: Date\n  high: number\n  low: number\n}\n\nexport const sfTemperatures: readonly DailyTemperature[] = [\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-01T00:00:00Z'), high: 68, low: 55 },\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-02T00:00:00Z'), high: 71, low: 56 },\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-03T00:00:00Z'), high: 66, low: 54 },\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-04T00:00:00Z'), high: 69, low: 55 },\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-05T00:00:00Z'), high: 73, low: 57 },\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-06T00:00:00Z'), high: 70, low: 56 },\n]","lang":"ts","meta":"group=temperature-range file=/src/data.ts collapsed","title":"/src/data.ts","file":"/src/data.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The numeric y axis uses the lightweight linear scale. The x axis upgrades to\nD3 UTC so spacing and ticks preserve elapsed time; install "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@types/d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" for that mapping."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-marks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom marks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMark"},{"type":"text","value":" when the needed geometry cannot be expressed by composing built-ins. A custom mark should still:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Materialize scale channels explicitly."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the resolved chart scales."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Emit stable keyed scene nodes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Emit typed interaction points when the geometry represents observations."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reuse theme, clipping, and renderer-neutral scene primitives."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most custom visualizations should remain compositions of built-in marks plus application-prepared rows. Reach for a custom mark only when the geometry itself is new. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/custom-marks-and-renderers.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Marks and Renderers"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"built-in-mark-families","text":"Built-in mark families","level":2},{"id":"layer-order-is-declaration-order","text":"Layer order is declaration order","level":2},{"id":"opt-individual-marks-into-canvas","text":"Opt individual marks into Canvas","level":2},{"id":"decorative-layers","text":"Decorative layers","level":2},{"id":"mark-identity","text":"Mark identity","level":2},{"id":"grouped-geometry","text":"Grouped geometry","level":2},{"id":"line-and-area-gaps","text":"Line and area gaps","level":2},{"id":"baselines-and-intervals","text":"Baselines and intervals","level":2},{"id":"style-values-and-visual-channels","text":"Style values and visual channels","level":2},{"id":"clipping","text":"Clipping","level":2},{"id":"complete-range-band-composition","text":"Complete range-band composition","level":2},{"id":"custom-marks","text":"Custom marks","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nA mark turns data and channel values into renderer-neutral scene nodes. Marks are small, composable units; a chart type is usually a useful arrangement of several marks rather than a separate component.\n\n## Built-in mark families\n\n| Visual task                                   | Start with                   |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| Trend or connected path                       | `lineY`                      |\n| Difference between two connected paths        | `differenceY`, `differenceX` |\n| Least-squares trend and confidence band       | `linearRegressionY`          |\n| Range, band, or filled trend                  | `areaY`, `areaX`             |\n| Category comparison                           | `barY`, `barX`               |\n| Interval, heatmap cell, or rectangular region | `rect`, `cell`               |\n| Relationship or individual observation        | `dot`, `hexagon`             |\n| Tukey distribution summary                    | `boxY`, `boxX`               |\n| Baseline, threshold, or reference             | `ruleX`, `ruleY`             |\n| Label or annotation                           | `text`                       |\n| Directed relationship                         | `arrow`, `link`, `vector`    |\n| Compact distribution glyph                    | `tickX`, `tickY`             |\n| Plot frame                                    | `frame`                      |\n| Small-multiple composition                    | `facet`, `facetChart`        |\n| Pie, donut, gauge, or cyclic profile          | `polar` and radial marks     |\n| Projected GeoJSON                             | `geoShape`                   |\n\nStart from the analytical question in\n[Choosing a Chart](../guides/choosing-a-chart.md). The\n[Mark Reference](../reference/index.md#mark-reference) lists every channel and\nstyle option. Polar and geographic marks use explicit capability subpaths.\n\n## Layer order is declaration order\n\nMarks earlier in the array paint behind later marks:\n\n```ts\nconst marks = [\n  areaY(rangeRows, rangeOptions),\n  ruleY([target], ruleOptions),\n  lineY(actualRows, lineOptions),\n  dot(highlightedRows, dotOptions),\n  text(labels, textOptions),\n]\n```\n\nA useful default order is:\n\n1. Background regions and filled areas\n2. Reference bands and rules\n3. Primary bars or lines\n4. Highlight dots, ticks, or vectors\n5. Labels and annotations\n\nThere is no separate overlay subsystem. An annotation is another mark with its own data, channels, and stable identity.\n\n## Opt individual marks into Canvas\n\nImport the Canvas renderer and attach it only to paint-heavy marks:\n\n```ts\nimport { canvasChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/canvas'\n\nconst marks = [\n  areaY(denseRange, {\n    x: 'date',\n    y1: 'low',\n    y2: 'high',\n    renderer: canvasChartRenderer,\n  }),\n  lineY(summary, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n  dot(highlights, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n  text(labels, { x: 'date', y: 'value', text: 'label' }),\n]\n```\n\nThe host keeps axes, guides, and marks without `renderer` in SVG. It creates\nordered SVG and Canvas layers from the mark declaration order, so a Canvas\nmark can sit behind, between, or in front of SVG marks. Focus, tooltips,\nkeyboard navigation, responsive updates, SSR shell adoption, and export still\nuse the shared chart host.\n\nCartesian and radial mark option objects accept `renderer`. A\n`compositeMark` can select a renderer for its complete output, or its children\ncan select their own renderers when the parent does not. The same nested\nselection works inside facets and `polar`.\n\nImporting `@tanstack/charts/canvas` is the opt-in boundary that adds the Canvas\npainter to the bundle. Reuse a stable renderer instance across updates. The\nexported `canvasChartRenderer` singleton already has stable identity. Creating\na new renderer or changing the renderer sequence replaces the affected layer\ncomposition.\n\n## Decorative layers\n\nWhen two layered marks describe the same observations, choose one interaction\nowner. For example, dots can own focus and tooltips while the connected line\nremains visual:\n\n```ts\nimport { dot, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { decorative } from '@tanstack/charts/mark/decorative'\n\nconst marks = [\n  decorative(lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' })),\n  dot(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n]\n```\n\n`decorative(mark)` preserves the mark's scale channels, domains, layout-label\nmeasurement, motion, and painted geometry. It removes interaction points and\nscene ownership, so the layer cannot add a second keyboard stop, tooltip, or\nactivation target. The input must be an always-painted mark without focus or\nstate behavior.\n\n## Mark identity\n\nEvery mark has an `id`. When omitted, it is derived from the mark type and array position.\n\nProvide an explicit `id` when:\n\n- Marks appear conditionally.\n- Marks reorder.\n- Two definitions should reconcile the same conceptual layer.\n- Application code needs a stable `markId` in interaction points.\n\n```ts\nlineY(rows, {\n  id: 'actual-revenue',\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'actual',\n})\n```\n\nBuilt-in marks infer datum identity from a unique top-level `id`, nested\n`data.id`, or mark-specific position. Supply `key` when that inferred value is\nnot the entity's stable identity.\n\n## Grouped geometry\n\nAn explicit `z` partitions geometry that should not connect:\n\n```ts\nlineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'value',\n  z: 'region',\n})\n```\n\nEach region becomes an independent line. Area marks use the same grouping\nrule. When `z` is omitted on a connected line or area, an authored `color`\nchannel supplies the path groups as well as color semantics. Explicit `z`\nalways wins when the two fields differ.\n\nBars stack their single quantitative channel by default. Use\n`layout: group({ scale })` for side-by-side bars; it groups by `z` when present\nand otherwise by discrete `color`. Explicit `y1`/`y2` or `x1`/`x2` channels\nopt out of implicit stacking and preserve authored intervals.\n\n## Line and area gaps\n\n`lineY` and `areaY` split geometry at missing or invalid positional values:\n\n```ts\ninterface Reading {\n  id: string\n  time: Date\n  low: number | null\n  high: number | null\n}\n\nareaY(readings, {\n  x: 'time',\n  y1: 'low',\n  y2: 'high',\n})\n```\n\nThe break is intentional evidence that no interval was materialized for that observation. Do not replace missing data with zero unless zero is semantically correct.\n\n## Baselines and intervals\n\nBar and area marks accept explicit endpoints:\n\n```ts\nareaY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y1: 'minimum',\n  y2: 'maximum',\n})\n\nbarY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y1: 'start',\n  y2: 'end',\n})\n```\n\nWhen `y1` or `x1` is omitted, bar and area baselines default to zero where that mark supports it. Supplying both endpoints makes the interval semantics explicit and includes both sides in scale materialization.\n\nRectangles are the general interval mark:\n\n```ts\nrect(windows, {\n  x1: 'start',\n  x2: 'end',\n  y1: 'minimum',\n  y2: 'maximum',\n})\n```\n\n## Style values and visual channels\n\nSome appearance options are constants:\n\n```ts\nlineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'value',\n  stroke: '#2563eb',\n  strokeWidth: 2.5,\n  strokeOpacity: 0.9,\n})\n```\n\nMarks that accept `VisualChannel` options can also derive a style from each row:\n\n```ts\nbarX(rows, {\n  x: 'value',\n  y: 'label',\n  fill: (row) => (row.highlighted ? '#f97316' : '#94a3b8'),\n})\n```\n\nUse a semantic group or color channel and configured color scale when color represents data consistently across observations. Use a visual accessor for local styling that is not a shared scale.\n\n## Clipping\n\nSet `clip: true` on the chart definition when marks must not paint outside the resolved plot rectangle:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  clip: true,\n})\n```\n\nClipping applies to the chart’s mark group, not axes or legends. Leave it off when an intentional annotation or marker should extend beyond the plot.\n\n## Complete range-band composition\n\n```ts group=temperature-range env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\nimport { areaY, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { sfTemperatures } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    areaY(sfTemperatures, {\n      id: 'daily-range',\n      x: 'date',\n      y1: 'low',\n      y2: 'high',\n      fill: '#60a5fa',\n      fillOpacity: 0.24,\n    }),\n    lineY(sfTemperatures, {\n      id: 'daily-low',\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'low',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n    lineY(sfTemperatures, {\n      id: 'daily-high',\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'high',\n      stroke: '#dc2626',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleUtc,\n      axis: { label: 'Day' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      nice: true,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Temperature (°F)' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=temperature-range file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport interface DailyTemperature {\n  date: Date\n  high: number\n  low: number\n}\n\nexport const sfTemperatures: readonly DailyTemperature[] = [\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-01T00:00:00Z'), high: 68, low: 55 },\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-02T00:00:00Z'), high: 71, low: 56 },\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-03T00:00:00Z'), high: 66, low: 54 },\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-04T00:00:00Z'), high: 69, low: 55 },\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-05T00:00:00Z'), high: 73, low: 57 },\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-06T00:00:00Z'), high: 70, low: 56 },\n]\n```\n\nThe numeric y axis uses the lightweight linear scale. The x axis upgrades to\nD3 UTC so spacing and ticks preserve elapsed time; install `d3-scale` and\n`@types/d3-scale` for that mapping.\n\n## Custom marks\n\nUse `createMark` when the needed geometry cannot be expressed by composing built-ins. A custom mark should still:\n\n- Materialize scale channels explicitly.\n- Use the resolved chart scales.\n- Emit stable keyed scene nodes.\n- Emit typed interaction points when the geometry represents observations.\n- Reuse theme, clipping, and renderer-neutral scene primitives.\n\nMost custom visualizations should remain compositions of built-in marks plus application-prepared rows. Reach for a custom mark only when the geometry itself is new. See [Custom Marks and Renderers](../guides/custom-marks-and-renderers.md).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Marks and Layering\ndescription: Choose built-in geometric marks, compose them in render order, and preserve stable layer identity.\n---\n\nA mark turns data and channel values into renderer-neutral scene nodes. Marks are small, composable units; a chart type is usually a useful arrangement of several marks rather than a separate component.\n\n## Built-in mark families\n\n| Visual task                                   | Start with                   |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| Trend or connected path                       | `lineY`                      |\n| Difference between two connected paths        | `differenceY`, `differenceX` |\n| Least-squares trend and confidence band       | `linearRegressionY`          |\n| Range, band, or filled trend                  | `areaY`, `areaX`             |\n| Category comparison                           | `barY`, `barX`               |\n| Interval, heatmap cell, or rectangular region | `rect`, `cell`               |\n| Relationship or individual observation        | `dot`, `hexagon`             |\n| Tukey distribution summary                    | `boxY`, `boxX`               |\n| Baseline, threshold, or reference             | `ruleX`, `ruleY`             |\n| Label or annotation                           | `text`                       |\n| Directed relationship                         | `arrow`, `link`, `vector`    |\n| Compact distribution glyph                    | `tickX`, `tickY`             |\n| Plot frame                                    | `frame`                      |\n| Small-multiple composition                    | `facet`, `facetChart`        |\n| Pie, donut, gauge, or cyclic profile          | `polar` and radial marks     |\n| Projected GeoJSON                             | `geoShape`                   |\n\nStart from the analytical question in\n[Choosing a Chart](../guides/choosing-a-chart.md). The\n[Mark Reference](../reference/index.md#mark-reference) lists every channel and\nstyle option. Polar and geographic marks use explicit capability subpaths.\n\n## Layer order is declaration order\n\nMarks earlier in the array paint behind later marks:\n\n```ts\nconst marks = [\n  areaY(rangeRows, rangeOptions),\n  ruleY([target], ruleOptions),\n  lineY(actualRows, lineOptions),\n  dot(highlightedRows, dotOptions),\n  text(labels, textOptions),\n]\n```\n\nA useful default order is:\n\n1. Background regions and filled areas\n2. Reference bands and rules\n3. Primary bars or lines\n4. Highlight dots, ticks, or vectors\n5. Labels and annotations\n\nThere is no separate overlay subsystem. An annotation is another mark with its own data, channels, and stable identity.\n\n## Opt individual marks into Canvas\n\nImport the Canvas renderer and attach it only to paint-heavy marks:\n\n```ts\nimport { canvasChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/canvas'\n\nconst marks = [\n  areaY(denseRange, {\n    x: 'date',\n    y1: 'low',\n    y2: 'high',\n    renderer: canvasChartRenderer,\n  }),\n  lineY(summary, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n  dot(highlights, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n  text(labels, { x: 'date', y: 'value', text: 'label' }),\n]\n```\n\nThe host keeps axes, guides, and marks without `renderer` in SVG. It creates\nordered SVG and Canvas layers from the mark declaration order, so a Canvas\nmark can sit behind, between, or in front of SVG marks. Focus, tooltips,\nkeyboard navigation, responsive updates, SSR shell adoption, and export still\nuse the shared chart host.\n\nCartesian and radial mark option objects accept `renderer`. A\n`compositeMark` can select a renderer for its complete output, or its children\ncan select their own renderers when the parent does not. The same nested\nselection works inside facets and `polar`.\n\nImporting `@tanstack/charts/canvas` is the opt-in boundary that adds the Canvas\npainter to the bundle. Reuse a stable renderer instance across updates. The\nexported `canvasChartRenderer` singleton already has stable identity. Creating\na new renderer or changing the renderer sequence replaces the affected layer\ncomposition.\n\n## Decorative layers\n\nWhen two layered marks describe the same observations, choose one interaction\nowner. For example, dots can own focus and tooltips while the connected line\nremains visual:\n\n```ts\nimport { dot, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { decorative } from '@tanstack/charts/mark/decorative'\n\nconst marks = [\n  decorative(lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' })),\n  dot(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n]\n```\n\n`decorative(mark)` preserves the mark's scale channels, domains, layout-label\nmeasurement, motion, and painted geometry. It removes interaction points and\nscene ownership, so the layer cannot add a second keyboard stop, tooltip, or\nactivation target. The input must be an always-painted mark without focus or\nstate behavior.\n\n## Mark identity\n\nEvery mark has an `id`. When omitted, it is derived from the mark type and array position.\n\nProvide an explicit `id` when:\n\n- Marks appear conditionally.\n- Marks reorder.\n- Two definitions should reconcile the same conceptual layer.\n- Application code needs a stable `markId` in interaction points.\n\n```ts\nlineY(rows, {\n  id: 'actual-revenue',\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'actual',\n})\n```\n\nBuilt-in marks infer datum identity from a unique top-level `id`, nested\n`data.id`, or mark-specific position. Supply `key` when that inferred value is\nnot the entity's stable identity.\n\n## Grouped geometry\n\nAn explicit `z` partitions geometry that should not connect:\n\n```ts\nlineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'value',\n  z: 'region',\n})\n```\n\nEach region becomes an independent line. Area marks use the same grouping\nrule. When `z` is omitted on a connected line or area, an authored `color`\nchannel supplies the path groups as well as color semantics. Explicit `z`\nalways wins when the two fields differ.\n\nBars stack their single quantitative channel by default. Use\n`layout: group({ scale })` for side-by-side bars; it groups by `z` when present\nand otherwise by discrete `color`. Explicit `y1`/`y2` or `x1`/`x2` channels\nopt out of implicit stacking and preserve authored intervals.\n\n## Line and area gaps\n\n`lineY` and `areaY` split geometry at missing or invalid positional values:\n\n```ts\ninterface Reading {\n  id: string\n  time: Date\n  low: number | null\n  high: number | null\n}\n\nareaY(readings, {\n  x: 'time',\n  y1: 'low',\n  y2: 'high',\n})\n```\n\nThe break is intentional evidence that no interval was materialized for that observation. Do not replace missing data with zero unless zero is semantically correct.\n\n## Baselines and intervals\n\nBar and area marks accept explicit endpoints:\n\n```ts\nareaY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y1: 'minimum',\n  y2: 'maximum',\n})\n\nbarY(rows, {\n  x: 'category',\n  y1: 'start',\n  y2: 'end',\n})\n```\n\nWhen `y1` or `x1` is omitted, bar and area baselines default to zero where that mark supports it. Supplying both endpoints makes the interval semantics explicit and includes both sides in scale materialization.\n\nRectangles are the general interval mark:\n\n```ts\nrect(windows, {\n  x1: 'start',\n  x2: 'end',\n  y1: 'minimum',\n  y2: 'maximum',\n})\n```\n\n## Style values and visual channels\n\nSome appearance options are constants:\n\n```ts\nlineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'value',\n  stroke: '#2563eb',\n  strokeWidth: 2.5,\n  strokeOpacity: 0.9,\n})\n```\n\nMarks that accept `VisualChannel` options can also derive a style from each row:\n\n```ts\nbarX(rows, {\n  x: 'value',\n  y: 'label',\n  fill: (row) => (row.highlighted ? '#f97316' : '#94a3b8'),\n})\n```\n\nUse a semantic group or color channel and configured color scale when color represents data consistently across observations. Use a visual accessor for local styling that is not a shared scale.\n\n## Clipping\n\nSet `clip: true` on the chart definition when marks must not paint outside the resolved plot rectangle:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  clip: true,\n})\n```\n\nClipping applies to the chart’s mark group, not axes or legends. Leave it off when an intentional annotation or marker should extend beyond the plot.\n\n## Complete range-band composition\n\n```ts group=temperature-range env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\nimport { areaY, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { sfTemperatures } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    areaY(sfTemperatures, {\n      id: 'daily-range',\n      x: 'date',\n      y1: 'low',\n      y2: 'high',\n      fill: '#60a5fa',\n      fillOpacity: 0.24,\n    }),\n    lineY(sfTemperatures, {\n      id: 'daily-low',\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'low',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n    lineY(sfTemperatures, {\n      id: 'daily-high',\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'high',\n      stroke: '#dc2626',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleUtc,\n      axis: { label: 'Day' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      nice: true,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Temperature (°F)' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=temperature-range file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport interface DailyTemperature {\n  date: Date\n  high: number\n  low: number\n}\n\nexport const sfTemperatures: readonly DailyTemperature[] = [\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-01T00:00:00Z'), high: 68, low: 55 },\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-02T00:00:00Z'), high: 71, low: 56 },\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-03T00:00:00Z'), high: 66, low: 54 },\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-04T00:00:00Z'), high: 69, low: 55 },\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-05T00:00:00Z'), high: 73, low: 57 },\n  { date: new Date('2026-07-06T00:00:00Z'), high: 70, low: 56 },\n]\n```\n\nThe numeric y axis uses the lightweight linear scale. The x axis upgrades to\nD3 UTC so spacing and ticks preserve elapsed time; install `d3-scale` and\n`@types/d3-scale` for that mapping.\n\n## Custom marks\n\nUse `createMark` when the needed geometry cannot be expressed by composing built-ins. A custom mark should still:\n\n- Materialize scale channels explicitly.\n- Use the resolved chart scales.\n- Emit stable keyed scene nodes.\n- Emit typed interaction points when the geometry represents observations.\n- Reuse theme, clipping, and renderer-neutral scene primitives.\n\nMost custom visualizations should remain compositions of built-in marks plus application-prepared rows. Reach for a custom mark only when the geometry itself is new. See [Custom Marks and Renderers](../guides/custom-marks-and-renderers.md).\n"}
